  "step_2": "The individual compared the information of the two lotteries and stated that both probabilities are low. The individual chose A because the probability of winning (0.2% vs 0.1%) was slightly higher in A. The justification is based solely on the higher winning probability (0.2% > 0.1%) and not on any consideration of the distribution of outcomes or skewness. The individual did not mention the 0 Euro outcomes, nor did they discuss the probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. There is no reference to skewness, right-skewness, or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. The deliberation process centers only on the winning probability being higher in A, which leads to the choice. Thus, the individual's reasoning is based on probability of success, not on skewness or distributional imbalance.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is present in the lotteries: B has a higher probability (99.9%) of yielding 0 Euros than A (99.8%), meaning B is more right-skewed. However, the individual did not consider this aspect at all. The individual's justification is based solely on the higher winning probability in A, not on skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason — which requires evaluating skewness and preferring the more right-skewed lottery. The reported reasoning is inconsistent with the decision reason. The individual failed to identify or apply the skewness criterion. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
